{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 21, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna and his uncle King Baldwin III of Jerusalem acted as regents for him until he came of age. During his minority the county was administered by the Knights Templar.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\n**Early Reign and Conflicts**\nIn 1164 Raymond III was captured by Nur ad-Din at the Battle of Harim and spent the next ten years in captivity. During his absence his cousin Bohemond III of Antioch took over the administration of Tripoli. Raymond was released in 1174 after paying a large ransom. Upon his return he resumed control of Tripoli and also became regent for Bohemond IIIs son Bohemond IV. Raymond played a key role in the politics of the Crusader states working closely with King Amalric of Jerusalem and later with Amalrics successor Baldwin IV. He was a leading figure in the baronial faction opposed to the growing power of the royal court and the influence of outsiders like Miles of Plancy and Aimery of Lusignan.\n\n**Later Years and Death**\nRaymond IIIs later years were marked by his opposition to Guy of Lusignan who became king of Jerusalem in 1186. Raymond supported Maria Comnena and her daughter Isabella as heirs to the throne instead of Guys wife Sibylla. He formed an alliance with Saladin and allowed Muslim forces to pass through his territory in 1187. This controversial move led to the Battle of Hattin where the Crusaders were decisively defeated. Raymond escaped the battle but died shortly afterward on 10 October 1187. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli as the region faced increased Muslim expansion under Saladin.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\n**Legacy**\nRaymond III is remembered as a capable ruler who navigated the complex politics of the Crusader states during a turbulent period. His alliance with Saladin remains controversial but was likely motivated by a desire to protect Tripoli from direct Muslim attacks. Raymonds death left a power vacuum that contributed to the decline of the County of Tripoli in the years leading up to the Third Crusade. His legacy is also tied to his role in the succession crisis in Jerusalem which had lasting consequences for the Crusader kingdoms.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.17142000794410706}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.3838888704776764}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -6.139089964563027e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.020960604771971703}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -5.4238757002167404e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -1.2993727978027891e-05}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.4013676941394806}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06803708523511887}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.0004663571889977902}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00039772229501977563}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.20008207857608795}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.1723317950963974}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -8.34461570775602e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009067714563570917}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.000552263343706727}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.34461570775602e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00015293381875380874}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.0011474461061879992}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.00019965562387369573}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.826049268245697}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.01827053166925907}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -0.00018749863374978304}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.0038682410959154367}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00011240804451517761}, {"token": "1", "log